<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Gascony</h1><p class="st-meta">602 CE – 1053 CE (approx.) · capital Bordeaux</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Duchy between the Garonne and the Pyrenees, set up by the Franks to contain the Basques, which became the core of Gascon identity.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 602 the Frankish kings created a Duchy of Vasconia under a duke named Genial to control the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a>, who had pushed north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> into the old Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novempopulania">Novempopulana</a>. The region soon slipped from Frankish control and was often allied with the dukes of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/early-duchy-of-aquitaine">Early Duchy of Aquitaine</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a> were notoriously hard to govern: in 778 they ambushed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne's</a> rearguard at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-roncevaux-pass">Roncevaux</a>. From the late 9th century a native dynasty, traditionally descended from the semi-legendary Sancho Mitarra, ruled largely independently. Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-sanchez-of-gascony">William Sánchez</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raiders and founded the abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-sever">Saint-Sever</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> became the ducal seat.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The male line ended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-vi-william-of-gascony">Sancho VI William</a> in 1032. After a succession struggle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-viii-duke-of-aquitaine">Guy-Geoffrey</a>, count of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-poitou">Poitou</a>, secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> in the 1050s and united it with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine</a>. Gascony passed with Aquitaine to the English kings and kept its own Occitan dialect.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-gascony.jpg" alt="Duchy of Gascony" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Armagnac.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>602 CE – 1053 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bordeaux</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Genial, William Sánchez, Sancho VI William</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Basque, Gascon Occitan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a>, <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duchy of Vasconia, Wasconia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=828&amp;focus=duchy-of-gascony" aria-label="Duchy of Gascony on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4666 593 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Gascony lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4986 849L5054 728M4986 849L5035 888M4986 849L5014 887M4986 849L5002 900M4986 849L4991 897"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M5035 888h0M5014 887h0M5002 900h0M4991 897h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4986 849h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4986 849h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bordeaux. <a href="/atlas?year=828&amp;focus=duchy-of-gascony">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
